  <article id="the-story">
    <section class="pi-opening" aria-labelledby="inside-introduction">
      <div class="pi-opening-inner">
        <p class="pi-kicker">INTRODUCTION / FROM THE AUTHOR</p>
        <h2 id="inside-introduction">IT WAS ROUGH<br>ON THE INSIDE.</h2>
        <div class="pi-opening-copy">
          <p>Here lies my jail journal excerpts. They reflect a journey of personal acceptance and self-love, spirituality and inner peace, radical acceptance and living with my circumstances, mental health and growing, standing on my own two legs, and overall learning about life.</p>
          <p>It was rough on the inside. I saw things I never want to see again, experienced things I never want to go through for the second time, and met people I never want to meet again. I documented it in my journals, and now I’m writing parts of it here.</p>
          <p>These journals do not tell every story about what happened around me. Most of the time I was writing about what was happening inside my head. Books were a refuge. The pages were a place to question everything, including myself. This is the journey they record.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-01" aria-labelledby="chapter-01-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>01</span> DECEMBER 2024</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-01-title">A SMALL LIBRARY IN A PLACE I DIDN’T WANT TO BE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>On December 14, I'd already been inside for weeks. My entry starts with group cleaning, a deputy working the evening shift, and the new book I was reading. That was often how it worked: the day's routine would show up on the page, and then my mind would go somewhere else entirely.</p>
<p>The bunk was crowded with books. Reading didn't make the jail disappear, but for a little while I could be somewhere other than Boulder County.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>My bunk is a small library — I've got eleven books in a cache. Books are my escape. Sometimes, when I'm deep in an author's narrative, I forget I'm even here.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>DECEMBER 14, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 001</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <aside class="pi-author-note"><strong>YOUR MEMORY GOES HERE / ARRIVAL</strong><p>[Describe the first days, the cell or dorm arrangement, intake, and the moments that stayed with you. The journal passages used here begin after you had already been inside. Don't add details you don't remember.]</p></aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-pink" id="chapter-02" aria-labelledby="chapter-02-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>02</span> DECEMBER 2024</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-02-title">THE WOMAN I THOUGHT NOBODY WOULD MISS.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Some of the earliest entries are brutal to read. I was preoccupied with who was noticing my absence, who would still speak to me, and whether there would be anyone waiting when I finally came home. I kept making predictions about being abandoned, then treating those predictions as if I already knew how the future would unfold.</p>
<p>Sometimes the words I used for myself were far crueler than anything anyone else had said to me. The journal doesn't hide that.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>So, I always end up there — I belong in the trash. I'm just someone to be thrown away &amp; discarded. I don't matter. I am completely convinced that most people wouldn't even notice if I left. Well, they won't even notice that I'm away for the winter. On the outside, I'm invisible.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>DECEMBER 14, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 002</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>But I know what's going to happen when I leave this place in a handful of months: Nobody will care when I return. I'll be finished with a tough time in life and there won't be anybody at the finish line saying, "Welcome home — you actually made it." Surely not saying, "What was it even like?" or "What did you learn?" Jail isn't a proud achievement, but I think surviving it is quite an accomplishment, isn't it?</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>DECEMBER 14, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 004</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>Those weren't two neat, separate problems. The fear that I had been forgotten and the conviction that I didn't deserve to be remembered fed each other. It would take much longer than a few pages to begin questioning either of them.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-03" aria-labelledby="chapter-03-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>03</span> JANUARY 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-03-title">LEARNING TO LOOK AT MYSELF DIFFERENTLY.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Books started giving me questions that weren't just variations of <em>what is wrong with me?</em> I was reading about shame and self-compassion. In January, I noticed an ugly double standard: I could be compassionate toward someone else's illness or addiction, while treating my own as evidence of a personal failure.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>If I were literally anybody else — not me — I'd have compassion, reflexively. Just like how I think that alcoholism is a disease for others, but it's a character flaw &amp; moral failing for me personally. I'm not unique, and my problems exist in millions of others — but why can't I just recognize that and see I'm not standing all by myself? It makes no sense to me.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>JANUARY 9, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 029</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I still feel like I don't exist, but I think that's just a part of being in jail — losing one's self-identity, losing one's sense of self altogether I think. When I'm out, I just hope that my identity returns. That my sense of belonging returns, because I am afraid I have been truly abandoned by those I thought would've been there for me.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>JANUARY 25, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 064</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>That insight didn't magically cure my loneliness. By the end of January I was still afraid of returning to a town where I imagined I'd been forgotten. But I was also trying, sometimes deliberately, to build a relationship with myself instead of waiting for somebody else to tell me I mattered.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-purple" id="chapter-04" aria-labelledby="chapter-04-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>04</span> FEBRUARY 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-04-title">BOOKS, QUESTIONS, AND A WAY TO SIT WITH MY OWN HEAD.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>I read constantly, moved between novels and books on recovery, and wrote my way through exercises on shame, mindfulness, and self-compassion. I also met with the jail chaplain and began asking serious questions about religion and spirituality. The reading opened up a different kind of escape: not just leaving the place mentally, but learning how to stay present without getting consumed by it.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Whoa — Chaplain is awesome! We talked for an hour and a half about religion, Christianity, sobriety, and spirituality. He's going to get me some Hindu texts &amp; the Dhammapada! And we're going to meet next week.</p>
          <p>I wonder where this spiritual journey will take me! I even grabbed a Bible (a good one) to study on my own time.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>FEBRUARY 17, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 180</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>I was reading the <em>Dhammapada</em>, exploring Hindu texts, and working through <em>Recovery Dharma</em>. Some days the dorm was so loud I wanted everyone to leave me alone. Other days, I was buried in books because I was genuinely curious what else I might learn.</p>
<aside class="pi-author-note"><strong>YOUR MEMORY GOES HERE / INSIDE THE JAIL</strong><p>[Add the firsthand incidents you want readers to know about: what the place looked and sounded like, what happened with the women, what you saw, and what you went through. The entries include some descriptions of routines and tensions, but they do not explain every event. Only you can fill these gaps.]</p></aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-yellow" id="chapter-05" aria-labelledby="chapter-05-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>05</span> FEBRUARY 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-05-title">MISSING THE WORLD OUTSIDE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Jamestown kept turning up in the journal. The creek, the pines, the mountains, my dog, the familiar streets—ordinary parts of life that felt very far away. In jail, I was surrounded by people and could still feel painfully alone. At home, I pictured solitude differently: chosen, familiar, and mine.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>In this moment, I am very homesick. I want to feel the grass on my bare feet, smell the pines, hear the creek, and let the breeze kiss me. I miss my friends — my neighbors — and Lou and sitting outside. In this moment, I'd do anything to be taken back.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>FEBRUARY 25, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 224</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>Missing home wasn't just about wanting out of jail. I missed the ordinary things I'd barely thought about before: grass under my feet, the sound of running water, and the freedom to decide where to go.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-06" aria-labelledby="chapter-06-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>06</span> MARCH 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-06-title">A LITTLE PEACE. NOT A PERFECT TRANSFORMATION.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>On March 13, something changed for a day. I wrote about feeling peaceful even with the noise, tension, and other people's moods around me. I wasn't claiming jail had become pleasant. I was finding out that I didn't have to react to every part of it.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>That's what the goal for the day is: don't let my vibe get killed. It's not like I feel okay with being in jail. But I am just able to do it and not let it bother the hell out of me. For one, I'm sick of reacting to jail. For two, I want inner peace &amp; if I can have it in jail, I think I have a good chance to maintain it on the outside.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>MARCH 13, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 262</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Something I should write is that I "ran" for a mentor position (1 of 2). Originally, I did it so that this one girl wouldn't get it, but now I'm not sure. 2 people told me that they voted for me and I have a definite 5 more votes... I never really expected to get it. Or even have people vote for me. I'm almost petrified to do it... but if my peers have confidence in me, I should too.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>MARCH 13, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 265</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>There were people inside I cared about, too. When women voted for me to be a mentor, I was surprised that they had confidence in me. Their faith in me clashed with the version of myself I had been writing about since December.</p>
<p>That feeling of peace did not last uninterrupted. Later entries still contain anger, loneliness, and thoughts about being unwanted. The progress was real, and so were the setbacks.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-purple" id="chapter-07" aria-labelledby="chapter-07-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>07</span> MARCH 26, 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-07-title">THE WORLD I COULD STILL PICTURE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Near the end of March, my mind kept returning to a particular place: Bluejay Mine and the old four-wheel-drive roads in the mountains. The descriptions of home became almost physical. I could picture the trees and hear the wind, even though I was nowhere near them.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I can almost perfectly see the forest if I close my eyes: the wind singing in the pines, the sun shining on the old 4x4 road, birds singing, not a soul around...
It's so far from this cinderblock compound with its harsh fluorescent lights &amp; sterile appearance.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>MARCH 26, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 285</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>That was the distance between the place I was stuck and the place I wanted to go. I was also making practical plans about work, transportation, and what living sober would mean after release.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-pink" id="chapter-08" aria-labelledby="chapter-08-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>08</span> APRIL 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-08-title">SOMETHING CAME AWAKE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>In the last weeks I was counting days and rehearsing life after release: work, therapy, sobriety, books, walking, and being home with Banjo. Then on April 4 I wrote about an unfamiliar feeling. I was starting to wonder whether I could see myself as something other than damaged or disposable.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>The feeling—this strange emotion—persists. I feel fresh, anew, &amp; like there is nothing that can touch me. Like I was blind &amp; can see everything so clearly. Like I didn't know, couldn't know better before. But over these past 4 1/2 months with almost nothing, I've come through. I was right when I told myself that the only way out is through. And I was right. I was abandoned, I was treated as subhuman, I grew up, I became social again. Something long asleep came awake inside of me.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 4, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 315</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>I hadn't become somebody else overnight. The journal still held the same questions and worries. But this entry sounds different from December. It says I had been hurt, had lived through it, and was beginning to see that surviving wasn't the same thing as being worthless.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-yellow" id="chapter-09" aria-labelledby="chapter-09-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>09</span> APRIL 10–11, 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-09-title">THE PART WHERE I STILL HAD TO CHOOSE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>I didn't leave jail with a perfect mindset or a guarantee about the future. Two days before release, I was still worried about returning to drinking and slipping back into old patterns. Therapy, mindfulness, and radical acceptance were things I had practiced, not boxes I could check off forever.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I got good advice during therapy. Basically be mindful and just acknowledge the passing of impermanent thoughts.
The alcoholic &amp; bipolar sides will unfortunately have eternal voices for the rest of my life. I just have to accept that, but I also have the choice to act on what they say. I can't ignore it, just have to let it go. I've been getting plenty of practice in jail...
Practicing radical acceptance is also something I've had a lot of practice with in jail. I never really wrestled with the sentence from the beginning.
So there it is.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 10, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 335</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I truly am wanting to start my life again. I came to jail just broken and dead inside. Somehow my passion for living came back. I'm trying to be an optimist now. I have so much hope, despite wanting to get rid of it earlier. I am hopeful for my future &amp; what it holds. I can't wait to see what's coming!</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 11, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 341</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>On April 11, I wrote about wanting my family to be proud and wanting to stay sober. The same pages also hold an enormous amount of hope. I hadn't forgotten why I was there; I was trying to decide how to live when I got out.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-10" aria-labelledby="chapter-10-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>10</span> APRIL 13, 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-10-title">THE DAY THE COUNTDOWN ENDED.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>On the last photographed morning, I was nervous and excited. I wrote about waiting for lockdown to end, drinking coffee with Carleen, and the farewell cake the women had made me the night before. I knew I would miss her. After months of writing about being forgotten, I was leaving with people I wanted to remember.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>It doesn't feel real at all. Like it'll just be another day in jail. OR there's some big mistake and I'll be stuck here. OR I'll have to wait hours for all of this to be over. When in 2 hours it'll most likely be over.
It still doesn't feel real.
Am I dreaming?</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 13, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL, PHOTO 347</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>That's where the photographed journal ends: before the actual moment I walked out, while I was still wondering if it could really be happening. I don't want to invent the rest of that morning just to give this story a tidy ending. The last question I wrote is enough—for now.</p>
<aside class="pi-author-note"><strong>YOUR MEMORY GOES HERE / RELEASE DAY</strong><p>[Describe the moment you were called to leave, walking outside, the ride home, and what you actually felt. These moments aren't recorded in the final supplied photograph.]</p></aside>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

